: 4.88, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": 8.1, "lean": 2.25, "environment": 4.3, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 1.55}, "rating_margin": 12.5, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.4, "baseline_margin": 11.07, "analyst_adjustment": -0.07, "key_factors": ["Klobuchar's double-digit polling lead, averaging about 11 points", "Massive fundraising edge: $7.0M raised vs. $0.4M for Demuth", "Prediction markets price Klobuchar at 94-98% to win", "Minnesota's 3-point Democratic lean in an open seat"], "rationale": "Every recent poll shows Klobuchar ahead by 8 to 18 points, with the latest surveys clustering around a 10-12 point margin, and she has vastly out-raised Demuth. Markets and most expert raters treat the race as effectively decided, so the forecast stays at a Safe D rating with roughly an 11-point expected margin.", "watch": "A late polling shift toward Demuth or turnout falling well below midterm norms in the Twin Cities metro could narrow the margin, though a Republican win would require an error far larger than typical polling misses.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Tim Walz (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's open governor's race pits Senator Amy Klobuchar against state Representative Lisa Demuth after Tim Walz's retirement. Klobuchar, a well-known statewide figure, has led every poll of the race, with late-October surveys from SurveyUSA, State Navigate, and NYT/Siena showing leads of 12 to 18 points. She has raised roughly $7 million to Demuth's $360,000, and outside money has flooded the contest in record amounts.\n\nExpert raters split between Solid D and Likely D, and prediction markets put Klobuchar's chances in the mid-90s. Demuth has consolidated the Republican base but has not closed the gap in any independent poll. The forecast expects Klobuchar to win comfortably, with the main uncertainty being the size of the margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9683, "margin": 10.03},